1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,267
A suitcase on wheels
glides with one finger.

2
00:00:03,267 --> 00:00:07,122
A steel cupboard, just as
heavy, needs two people to drag.

3
00:00:07,522 --> 00:00:10,169
Why does rolling make
heavy things so easy?

4
00:00:10,619 --> 00:00:13,852
On his way to college,
Aarav's back tyre goes flat.

5
00:00:14,202 --> 00:00:18,537
Same level road, yet every push
on the pedal feels like a hill.

6
00:00:18,537 --> 00:00:19,437
Why?

7
00:00:19,553 --> 00:00:21,553
The answer is rolling friction.

8
00:00:22,153 --> 00:00:23,945
So what is rolling friction?

9
00:00:24,295 --> 00:00:28,626
When a body rolls over a surface,
the force that opposes the rolling is

10
00:00:28,626 --> 00:00:29,790
rolling friction.

11
00:00:30,190 --> 00:00:34,667
A perfectly hard wheel on a perfectly
hard floor touches at one point,

12
00:00:34,667 --> 00:00:36,799
and that point does not slide.

13
00:00:36,799 --> 00:00:38,255
No friction at all.

14
00:00:38,655 --> 00:00:42,265
But real wheels and floors
squash a little where they meet.

15
00:00:42,265 --> 00:00:44,603
That squashing causes rolling friction.

16
00:00:45,253 --> 00:00:46,810
To the motion notebook.

17
00:00:46,810 --> 00:00:49,308
A fifty kilogram box on a rough floor.

18
00:00:49,658 --> 00:00:54,123
To keep it sliding steadily, Aarav
pulls with one hundred and ninety six

19
00:00:54,123 --> 00:00:55,023
newtons.

20
00:00:54,823 --> 00:00:56,736
Exactly the kinetic friction.

21
00:00:57,136 --> 00:00:58,036
More load.

22
00:00:58,002 --> 00:01:01,878
Seventy five kilograms, two
hundred and ninety four newtons.

23
00:01:01,878 --> 00:01:04,737
One hundred, three hundred and ninety two.

24
00:01:05,137 --> 00:01:07,528
Now the same box on a light trolley.

25
00:01:07,528 --> 00:01:10,496
The pull drops to just
four point nine newtons.

26
00:01:10,896 --> 00:01:12,299
Add the same loads.

27
00:01:12,299 --> 00:01:15,445
At one hundred kilograms,
only nine point eight.

28
00:01:15,845 --> 00:01:19,223
Both graph lines are straight:
more load, more friction.

29
00:01:19,223 --> 00:01:21,835
But the rolling line
is forty times flatter.

30
00:01:22,435 --> 00:01:23,504
Slow-mo replay.

31
00:01:23,504 --> 00:01:25,861
Freeze, and look under one wheel.

32
00:01:26,211 --> 00:01:28,367
The floor and the tyre both squash.

33
00:01:28,367 --> 00:01:31,263
The wheel rests on a
small patch, not a point.

34
00:01:31,663 --> 00:01:36,288
The floor pushes up from the front of
that patch, so its push leans backwards.

35
00:01:36,288 --> 00:01:38,587
That backward part is rolling friction.

36
00:01:39,237 --> 00:01:41,365
Now the rule, one step at a time.

37
00:01:41,715 --> 00:01:42,615
Step one.

38
00:01:42,615 --> 00:01:46,356
On a level floor, the
normal force N equals m g.

39
00:01:47,106 --> 00:01:48,006
Step two.

40
00:01:48,003 --> 00:01:49,338
Sliding friction.

41
00:01:49,338 --> 00:01:51,383
f k equals mu k times N.

42
00:01:52,133 --> 00:01:53,205
Step three.

43
00:01:53,205 --> 00:01:58,018
Rolling friction has the same
form: f r equals mu r times N.

44
00:01:58,018 --> 00:01:59,649
Mu r has no unit.

45
00:02:00,399 --> 00:02:01,448
Step four.

46
00:02:01,448 --> 00:02:03,244
Same load, same N.

47
00:02:03,244 --> 00:02:06,704
So f r over f k equals mu r over mu k.

48
00:02:07,454 --> 00:02:08,394
Step five.

49
00:02:08,394 --> 00:02:10,802
Mu r is far smaller than mu k.

50
00:02:10,802 --> 00:02:13,351
So rolling friction is far smaller.

51
00:02:14,101 --> 00:02:15,138
Remember this.

52
00:02:15,138 --> 00:02:19,761
For the same load, rolling friction
is much smaller than sliding friction.

53
00:02:20,461 --> 00:02:22,753
Now, back to Aarav's flat tyre.

54
00:02:22,753 --> 00:02:23,873
Link by link.

55
00:02:24,223 --> 00:02:27,282
A flat tyre squashes much
more against the road.

56
00:02:27,682 --> 00:02:31,731
A bigger squash means a bigger mu
r, so a bigger rolling friction.

57
00:02:32,131 --> 00:02:35,276
To keep the same speed,
Aarav must push harder.

58
00:02:35,276 --> 00:02:37,781
That extra push is the hill he feels.

59
00:02:38,431 --> 00:02:39,488
Side by side.

60
00:02:39,488 --> 00:02:42,501
Sliding: the whole bottom
of the box scrapes.

61
00:02:42,501 --> 00:02:44,312
Tiny bumps catch and tear.

62
00:02:44,712 --> 00:02:48,848
Rolling: each part of the wheel
touches down and lifts straight off.

63
00:02:48,848 --> 00:02:49,930
Nothing scrapes.

64
00:02:49,930 --> 00:02:51,550
Only the squash is left.

65
00:02:52,150 --> 00:02:53,126
Now you try.

66
00:02:53,126 --> 00:02:56,947
A steel ball rolls on a soft
carpet, and on a steel plate.

67
00:02:56,947 --> 00:02:59,279
Where is the rolling friction bigger?

68
00:02:59,279 --> 00:03:00,493
Pause and decide.

69
00:03:03,693 --> 00:03:04,758
On the carpet!

70
00:03:04,758 --> 00:03:07,857
The soft carpet squashes
much more under the ball.

71
00:03:07,857 --> 00:03:10,118
More squash, more rolling friction.

72
00:03:10,768 --> 00:03:12,869
Friction is not always the enemy.

73
00:03:12,869 --> 00:03:17,070
Your foot pushes back on the ground,
and friction pushes you forward.

74
00:03:17,470 --> 00:03:18,371
Your turn.

75
00:03:18,371 --> 00:03:22,876
On a perfectly frictionless road,
could Aarav's bicycle start moving?

76
00:03:22,876 --> 00:03:24,202
Pause and decide.

77
00:03:27,402 --> 00:03:28,302
No!

78
00:03:27,845 --> 00:03:29,929
The back wheel would just spin.

79
00:03:29,929 --> 00:03:33,691
With no friction, the road
cannot push the bicycle forward.

80
00:03:33,691 --> 00:03:35,370
Brakes need friction too.

81
00:03:36,020 --> 00:03:40,280
Where friction wastes energy, we cut
it: oil, and ball bearings that turn

82
00:03:40,280 --> 00:03:41,598
sliding into rolling.

83
00:03:42,198 --> 00:03:43,934
Now, one worked example.

84
00:03:44,284 --> 00:03:49,402
A ten tonne wagon rolls on rails,
with mu r of zero point zero zero two.

85
00:03:49,402 --> 00:03:51,267
Find the rolling friction.

86
00:03:51,267 --> 00:03:54,822
And if it skidded, with
mu k of zero point four?

87
00:03:55,322 --> 00:03:58,786
N is ten thousand kilograms
times nine point eight.

88
00:03:58,786 --> 00:04:00,850
Ninety eight thousand newtons.

89
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:06,058
Rolling: zero point zero zero
two times ninety eight thousand.

90
00:04:06,058 --> 00:04:08,656
One hundred and ninety six newtons.

91
00:04:09,406 --> 00:04:12,961
Skidding: zero point four
times ninety eight thousand.

92
00:04:12,961 --> 00:04:15,640
Thirty nine thousand two hundred newtons.

93
00:04:16,390 --> 00:04:18,135
Two hundred times more.

94
00:04:18,135 --> 00:04:23,285
Ten tonnes on wheels needs the same
pull as our fifty kilogram box sliding.

95
00:04:23,935 --> 00:04:25,400
Now, the exam trap.

96
00:04:25,750 --> 00:04:30,245
Many students say rolling friction is
zero, because the contact point does

97
00:04:30,245 --> 00:04:31,145
not slide.

98
00:04:31,501 --> 00:04:34,614
That is true only for
a perfectly hard wheel.

99
00:04:34,614 --> 00:04:39,181
Real surfaces squash, so rolling
friction is small, but never zero.

100
00:04:39,831 --> 00:04:41,264
Where is this asked?

101
00:04:41,264 --> 00:04:44,408
In questions comparing
sliding and rolling friction.

102
00:04:45,008 --> 00:04:46,259
Exam level now.

103
00:04:46,259 --> 00:04:50,795
A sixty kilogram crate slides on
a floor, mu k zero point five.

104
00:04:50,795 --> 00:04:55,501
It is put on a twenty kilogram
trolley, mu r zero point zero two.

105
00:04:55,501 --> 00:04:56,433
g is ten.

106
00:04:56,433 --> 00:04:59,920
By what factor does the
steady pushing force fall?

107
00:04:59,920 --> 00:05:01,224
Pause and solve.

108
00:05:06,624 --> 00:05:08,025
The answer is B.

109
00:05:08,025 --> 00:05:10,361
Eighteen point seven five times.

110
00:05:10,861 --> 00:05:14,948
Sliding: N is six hundred newtons,
so the force is three hundred.

111
00:05:15,448 --> 00:05:18,233
On the trolley, the load
is eighty kilograms.

112
00:05:18,233 --> 00:05:21,026
N is eight hundred, so
the force is sixteen.

113
00:05:21,526 --> 00:05:23,293
Three hundred over sixteen.

114
00:05:23,293 --> 00:05:26,454
Twenty five is the trap:
it forgets the trolley.

115
00:05:27,054 --> 00:05:28,512
Here is your recap.

116
00:05:28,512 --> 00:05:30,012
Screenshot this card.

117
00:05:33,212 --> 00:05:35,142
Can you answer these without help?

118
00:05:35,542 --> 00:05:36,442
One.

119
00:05:36,065 --> 00:05:40,036
Why does a flat tyre make
cycling harder on the same road?

120
00:05:40,936 --> 00:05:41,836
Two.

121
00:05:41,471 --> 00:05:46,322
Why is rolling friction not zero,
if the contact point does not slide?

122
00:05:47,222 --> 00:05:48,122
Three.

123
00:05:47,809 --> 00:05:50,974
How do ball bearings reduce
friction in a machine?

124
00:05:52,075 --> 00:05:54,258
Next time: a block on a slope.

125
00:05:54,258 --> 00:05:57,432
What pulls it down, and
how fast does it slide?

126
00:05:57,432 --> 00:05:59,154
Motion on inclined planes.
